/* ============================================================ 7. Preprocessing */
{
  const s = lightSlide(1, "Preprocessing");
  title(s, "Preprocessing: deliberately minimal");
  const cw3 = (CW - 0.5) / 2;
  card(s, MX, 2.15, cw3, 2.5, TINT);
  cardHead(s, MX, 2.15, cw3, "Done on load");
  cardBody(s, MX, 2.15, cw3, 1.7,
    "Explicit dtypes, which halves the memory and puts the schema intent in code. One timestamp built from Date and Time, then a global sort by time. No missing values in the raw data, so no imputation. The only NaN is an entity feature on an account's first-ever transaction, and it means “no history”, not “missing”.");
  card(s, MX + cw3 + 0.5, 2.15, cw3, 2.5, WHITE);
  cardHead(s, MX + cw3 + 0.5, 2.15, cw3, "Differs by model", MUTE);
  cardBody(s, MX + cw3 + 0.5, 2.15, cw3, 1.7,
    "LightGBM: categoricals kept as a native category dtype, no one-hot. Trees are scale-invariant, so no scaling. The one transform kept is log1p on the amount, purely for readable feature importance.\n\n" +
    "Baseline (Logistic Regression): median-impute, standardise, one-hot, because linear models require it.");
  bullets(s, [
    "No target encoding, no PCA. They add leakage surface and opacity for no measurable gain here.",
    "The result: the tree does the work. That is exactly why the linear baseline is so much weaker.",
  ], { y: 5.05, fontSize: 13 });
  note(s, "Preprocessing is minimal for the tree by design. The heavy prep, scaling and one-hot, is only for the linear baseline. The NaN in the entity features is meaningful: it means the account has no history, not that a value is missing.");
}

/* ============================================================ 8. Feature engineering */
{
  const s = lightSlide(1, "Feature engineering");
  title(s, "Feature engineering: behaviour is the signal");
  const fcw = (CW - 0.4) / 2;
  card(s, MX, 2.05, fcw, 1.9, TINT);
  cardHead(s, MX, 2.05, fcw, "Transaction-level  ·  single row, no leakage risk");
  cardBody(s, MX, 2.05, fcw, 1.3, "log-amount, amount bands, cross-border, currency-mismatch, hour, weekday");
  card(s, MX + fcw + 0.4, 2.05, fcw, 1.9, TINT);
  cardHead(s, MX + fcw + 0.4, 2.05, fcw, "Entity-level  ·  causal, per sender and receiver");
  cardBody(s, MX + fcw + 0.4, 2.05, fcw, 1.3, "prior txn count, time since last txn, trailing 1d / 7d / 30d counts, 7d sum and mean, amount vs. the account's recent norm");
  bullets(s, [
    "Corridor flags encode a laundering move: each border or currency hop breaks the trail. Cheap, a clean split point, and they keep the per-alert reason readable.",
    "Windows chosen for meaning: 1 day catches a burst, 7 days the account's rhythm, 30 days its baseline. Every one is strictly backward-looking (next slide).",
    "Excluded on purpose: account IDs (identifiers, not behaviour) and Laundering_type (a post-hoc label, so target leakage).",
  ], { y: 4.35, fontSize: 13, gap: 10 });
  note(s, "Two groups. Transaction-level features come from one row, so no leakage risk. Entity-level features describe how the account was behaving before this transaction. The corridor flags are cheap and interpretable but not load-bearing. The real signal is behavioural: a transaction many times the account's own recent norm. Windows are chosen for meaning, not tuned. And I deliberately exclude account IDs and Laundering_type.");
}

/* ============================================================ 9. Leakage prevention */
{
  const s = lightSlide(1, "Feature engineering");
  title(s, "Leakage prevention: the features cannot see the future");
  s.addShape(p.ShapeType.roundRect, { x: MX, y: 1.95, w: CW, h: 1.95, rectRadius: 0.04, fill: { color: INK } });
  s.addText(
    [
      { text: "# frame is globally time-sorted on load", options: { color: "B7B7B7", breakLine: true, paraSpaceAfter: 6 } },
      { text: 'g = df.set_index("timestamp").groupby("Sender_account")["Amount"]', options: { color: WHITE, breakLine: true, paraSpaceAfter: 6 } },
      { text: 'df["sender_cnt_7d"] = g.rolling("7D", ', options: { color: WHITE } },
      { text: 'closed="left"', options: { color: "F6C177" } },
      { text: ").count()", options: { color: WHITE, breakLine: true, paraSpaceAfter: 6 } },
      { text: '#   closed="left" keeps the window [t-7d, t), so the current transaction is excluded', options: { color: "B7B7B7" } },
    ],
    { x: MX + 0.35, y: 2.2, w: CW - 0.7, h: 1.5, fontFace: "Courier New", fontSize: 11.5, isTextBox: true, margin: 0, lineSpacingMultiple: 1.0 },
  );
  bullets(s, [
    "closed=\"left\" makes the window [t-7d, t). It stops the instant before the current transaction, so nothing at time t or after can enter it.",
    "Expanding counts use cumcount(), so only rows already seen. An account's first-ever transaction gets count 0, not 1.",
    "Locked by a test: a 3-row toy account asserts the first transaction sees count 0, an empty window, and a null time-since-last. Break causality and the test fails in CI.",
    "Why it matters: train on future information and the model looks excellent in testing, then collapses in production. It is the most common silent ML failure.",
  ], { y: 4.3, fontSize: 13, gap: 10 });
  note(s, "This is the one place I would expect a deep dive. Every history feature answers a question about what happened before this transaction, enforced three ways: a global sort on load, closed-left on the rolling window, and cumcount for expanding counts. A unit test on a toy account locks it, so a future change cannot quietly reintroduce leakage.");
}

/* ============================================================ 10. Splitting the data */
{
  const s = lightSlide(1, "Splitting the data");
  title(s, "Splitting train, validation and test, strictly by time");
  const months = ["Oct", "Nov", "Dec", "Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr", "May", "Jun", "Jul", "Aug"];
  const bx = MX, cw = CW / 11, by = 2.35, ch = 1.0;
  months.forEach((m, i) => {
    const kind = i < 8 ? INK : i === 8 ? SLATE : CORAL;
    s.addShape(p.ShapeType.rect, { x: bx + i * cw + 0.03, y: by, w: cw - 0.1, h: ch, fill: { color: kind }, line: { type: "none" } });
    s.addText(m, { x: bx + i * cw, y: by + 0.32, w: cw, h: 0.35, align: "center", fontFace: BODY, fontSize: 10.5, bold: true, color: WHITE, isTextBox: true, margin: 0 });
  });
  groupBrace(s, bx + 0.03, 8 * cw - 0.1, by + ch + 0.14, "TRAINING\n8 months  ·  Oct 2022 to May 2023", INK);
  groupBrace(s, bx + 8 * cw + 0.03, cw - 0.1, by + ch + 0.14, "VALIDATION\nJune  ·  threshold only", SLATE);
  groupBrace(s, bx + 9 * cw + 0.03, 2 * cw - 0.1, by + ch + 0.14, "TEST\nJul + Aug  ·  touched once", CORAL);
  bullets(s, [
    "No shuffling. A random split lets August behaviour inform an October prediction, which inflates the score and hides model decay.",
    "Rejected alternative: account-grouped k-fold. It removes account leakage but still mixes time periods.",
    "Entity features are computed over the full timeline before splitting, so validation and test rows carry real history, exactly as at inference. Encoders are fit on train only.",
  ], { y: 4.85, fontSize: 12.5, gap: 9 });
  note(s, "Strictly by time. A random split is leakage and it hides decay. Account-grouped k-fold, the sensible-sounding alternative, still mixes time periods. Train is the first eight months, validation is June and is used only to pick the threshold, and test is July and August and is touched exactly once.");
}

/* ============================================================ 11. Class imbalance */
{
  const s = lightSlide(1, "Class imbalance");
  title(s, "Handling class imbalance: a threshold problem");
  pic(s, "sweep.png", { x: MX, y: 2.05, w: 6.7, h: 4.5 });
  s.addText([
    { text: "chart data pulled live from MLflow:  ", options: { color: MUTE } },
    { text: 'mlflow.search_runs(experiment_names=["aml-part1"])', options: { color: INK, fontFace: "Courier New" } },
  ], { x: MX, y: 6.6, w: 7.2, h: 0.35, fontFace: BODY, fontSize: 9.5, italic: true, isTextBox: true, margin: 0 });
  bullets(s, [
    "Three options weighed: resample (SMOTE or undersample), cost-weight the loss, or leave the loss alone and move the threshold",
    "The obvious cost-weight, the roughly 1000x imbalance ratio, collapses PR-AUC below the linear baseline of 0.012. It floods the alert queue with borderline negatives.",
    "ROC-AUC barely moves (0.86 to 0.99), so it is the wrong metric to steer by",
    "SMOTE rejected: interpolating categorical and count features invents impossible rows, and it has no place in a time-ordered split",
  ], { x: 7.5, y: 2.15, w: CW - 6.9, fontSize: 12.5 });
  note(s, "The slide I most want to discuss, because the obvious answer is wrong. This chart is not hand-made: it is read straight out of MLflow with mlflow.search_runs on the aml-part1 experiment, the five runs from one command. Weighting the positive class by the imbalance ratio makes PR-AUC about eighty times worse, below the linear baseline. The model already ranks well, so all the weighting does is push borderline negatives up the queue. Notice ROC-AUC barely moves, which previews why it is the wrong metric. I ship weight one and handle the real trade-off at the threshold.");
}

